{"id": "FC1-c050", "lesson": "L03", "front": "Under B1 to B4, why does the symmetric profile (c+k, c+k) with k ≥ 3 fail to be an equilibrium?", "back": "A dealer undercuts by one cent, to c+k−1, and wins all N shares, earning (k−1)N. At the tie it earns kN/2, and (k−1)N is strictly larger exactly when k > 2 (Proposition 3.2).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "FC1-c056", "lesson": "L04", "front": "What is a market order, and what happens to its unfilled shares?", "back": "A market order trades at the best prices available on the opposite side at arrival, taking resting orders in price-time priority. Shares it cannot fill are cancelled, and it never rests in the book (Definition 1.1)."}
{"id": "FC1-c057", "lesson": "L04", "front": "What is a limit order?", "back": "An order with a price limit p. A buy limit trades only at prices at most p, a sell limit only at prices at least p, and unfilled shares rest in the book at p (Definition 1.1)."}
{"id": "FC1-c058", "lesson": "L04", "front": "When does a buy stop activate?", "back": "A buy stop activates when the last trade price is at or above its stop price. On activation it becomes a market order for its full quantity (Definition 1.1)."}
{"id": "FC1-c059", "lesson": "L04", "front": "At what price does a fill occur?", "back": "At the resting order's price, not the incoming order's (Remark 1.2)."}
{"id": "FC1-c060", "lesson": "L04", "front": "When are pending stops checked?", "back": "Only after the arriving order has finished matching, not after each fill of that order (Remark 1.2)."}
{"id": "FC1-c061", "lesson": "L04", "front": "What is the cost in cents of a market buy of 150 shares that meets 60 shares at 1000 cents and 100 shares at 1100 cents?", "back": "159000 cents: 60 shares at 1000 and 90 shares at 1100 (Example 1.3; Lab 1, Fixture A).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "FC1-c062", "lesson": "L04", "front": "In Example 1.4, why does a sell stop at 990 cents fill at 985 cents?", "back": "A market buy fills at 990, so the last trade price is 990. That is at or below the sell stop's price of 990, so the stop activates as a market sell. It fills 100 shares at the best bid, 985 cents (Example 1.4; Lab 1, Fixture B).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "FC1-c063", "lesson": "L04", "front": "What is the limit order book?", "back": "The set of resting limit orders on each side. The bid side is ordered from highest price to lowest, and the ask side from lowest to highest (Definition 2.1)."}
{"id": "FC1-c064", "lesson": "L04", "front": "What is price-time priority?", "back": "An incoming order meets the best price on the opposite side first. Among resting orders at that price it meets the earliest arrival first (Definition 2.1)."}
{"id": "FC1-c065", "lesson": "L04", "front": "When is the book crossed?", "back": "The book is crossed when the best bid is at least the best ask (Definition 2.1)."}
{"id": "FC1-c066", "lesson": "L04", "front": "What does share conservation say about the entered shares G, resting shares R, traded shares X and cancelled shares C?", "back": "Under A2 (no withdrawals), G = R + 2X + C after every event, where C is the shares cancelled from market orders (Proposition 2.3).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "FC1-c067", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Why does the traded term in share conservation carry a factor of two?", "back": "Each fill of q shares adds q to the filled quantity of both the incoming order and the resting order, so every traded share is counted in two orders. A single fill of 60 shares contributes 120 to the filled sum (Remark 2.4; Problem R3)."}
{"id": "FC1-c068", "lesson": "L04", "front": "If a book starts with a best bid of 1005 cents and a best ask of 1000 cents, a start that Assumption A1 excludes, is it crossed after a later 5-share limit buy at 999 cents?", "back": "Yes. The buy at 999 does not trade and rests as a bid. The best bid stays 1005, which is still at least the best ask of 1000, so the book is crossed (Problem R2).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "FC1-c069", "lesson": "L04", "front": "In the 300-event SIMULATED flow from seed 2026, how many events left the book crossed, and how many failed share conservation?", "back": "None of either. Lab 1 prints 0 crossed events and 0 conservation failures for this run (SIMULATED, seed 2026).", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (SIMULATED, seed 2026)"}
{"id": "FC1-c070", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Does a market order fill at the price on the screen?", "back": "No. It fills at the resting prices it meets, in order. Only the shares that meet the best ask fill at its price, and the rest fill at the other resting prices (misconception 1)."}
{"id": "FC1-c071", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Does a stop order sell at its stop price?", "back": "No. A stop becomes a market order on activation and fills at prices on the opposite side, not at its stop price (misconception 2)."}
{"id": "FC1-c072", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Does price-time priority fill the largest order first at the best price?", "back": "No. Priority is by arrival at a price, and size plays no part. If a 10-share sell and a 100-share sell rest at the same price, with the 10-share sell arriving first, a market buy of 10 shares fills the 10-share sell (misconception 3)."}
{"id": "FC1-c073", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What is clearing?", "back": "Clearing fixes the terms of an executed trade and computes what each party owes. It confirms the quantity, price, instrument and counterparties (Definition 1.1)."}
{"id": "FC1-c074", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What does a central counterparty become in a novated trade?", "back": "It becomes the buyer to the seller and the seller to the buyer for each trade it clears (Definition 1.1)."}
{"id": "FC1-c075", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What are the six states of the trade lifecycle, in order?", "back": "Order entry, matching, trade capture, clearing, settlement and position update. Each carries a business-day offset from the trade date. In this model order entry, matching and trade capture have offset 0 (Definition 1.2)."}
{"id": "FC1-c076", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What is a business day, and is a holiday calendar used?", "back": "A business day is a Monday to Friday. No holiday calendar is used (Definition 1.2)."}
{"id": "FC1-c077", "lesson": "L05", "front": "Do the six states and their offsets describe any real venue or clearing house?", "back": "No. They describe a model of a trade. The clearing offset and the settlement lag are parameters that Lab 1 sets, and no timing fact for any venue is stated (Remark 1.3)."}
{"id": "FC1-c078", "lesson": "L05", "front": "How is the consideration of a trade in a batch defined?", "back": "Trade i has consideration c_i = q_i p_i, its quantity times its price, in integer cents (Section 2)."}
{"id": "FC1-c079", "lesson": "L05", "front": "Under A1, what do the net positions of a batch sum to?", "back": "Zero. Each trade adds its consideration to the seller and subtracts the same amount from the buyer. A1 requires that the buyer and the seller of each trade differ and that quantities and prices are positive integers (Proposition 2.1(i)).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "FC1-c080", "lesson": "L05", "front": "Under A1 and A2, what is the CCP's net cash over a batch?", "back": "Zero. A2 requires that every trade is novated to the CCP, that the CCP charges no fee, and that it is party to no trade outside the batch (Proposition 2.2).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "FC1-c081", "lesson": "L05", "front": "Do the netting identities show that every payment is made?", "back": "No. They are identities about obligations. The model has no fees, margin or default, and it states no rule for what happens when a payment fails (Remark 2.3)."}
{"id": "FC1-c082", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What is the Lab 1 trade, in shares and cents per share?", "back": "One fill of 216 shares at 10060 cents, the resting sell's price. SIMULATED.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (SIMULATED)"}
{"id": "FC1-c083", "lesson": "L05", "front": "In Lab 1, on what date does a trade of Wednesday 2026-10-28 settle with a lag of 2 business days?", "back": "Friday 2026-10-30. Thursday is offset 1 and Friday is offset 2 (Lab 1, settlement state). SIMULATED.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (SIMULATED; the lag is a parameter)"}
{"id": "FC1-c084", "lesson": "L05", "front": "In the Lab 1 batch of 20 trades, what are net payments as a share of gross payments?", "back": "0.209 on the gross basis. Net payments are 14282414 cents and gross payments are 68398886 cents. The quotient is 0.20881062 (0.20881 to five decimals), rounded half up to three decimals (Lab 1). SIMULATED.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (SIMULATED)"}
{"id": "FC1-c085", "lesson": "L05", "front": "Does settlement happen when the trade is matched?", "back": "No. In Lab 1 matching is at offset 0 and settlement is at offset 2. The gap is the SETTLEMENT_LAG parameter, not a fact about any market (misconception 1).", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 state lines (SIMULATED parameter)"}
{"id": "FC1-c086", "lesson": "L05", "front": "Does netting cancel the payments, so that a batch nets to zero cash?", "back": "No. The net positions sum to zero, but the net payments are not zero. Lab 1 prints 14282414 cents of net payments against 68398886 cents of gross payments (misconception 2).", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (SIMULATED), netted batch of 20 trades"}
{"id": "FC1-c087", "lesson": "L05", "front": "Does a CCP with a zero net position carry no risk?", "back": "Not on this model's evidence. The zero is an identity under A2, which excludes fees and trades outside the batch, and the model has no margin and no default. A zero net position is not a measure of exposure (misconception 3)."}
{"id": "FC1-c088", "lesson": "L05", "front": "Is a trade struck at the buyer's limit price?", "back": "No. In Lab 1 the buy's limit is 10117 cents, and the trade is at the resting sell's price of 10060 cents. The limit is a ceiling the buyer will accept (misconception 4).", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (SIMULATED), one fill"}
{"id": "FC1-c089", "lesson": "L05", "front": "Does the bound NP ≤ G hold when one consideration is negative?", "back": "No. With one trade of consideration −100 cents, net payments are 100 cents and gross payments are −100 cents, so the bound 100 ≤ −100 is false. The step Pay_j ≥ 0 in the proof of Proposition 2.1(ii) needs non-negative considerations.", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "FC1-c090", "lesson": "L05", "front": "Why is no settlement period stated for any market?", "back": "Because no source for a settlement cycle is cited. The two-day lag of Lab 1 is a parameter, not a period for any market (Section 4)."}
{"id": "FC1-c099", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Can the book be crossed after an event when it starts empty?", "back": "No. Under A1, the book starts empty, and under the matching rules of Section 1 it is not crossed after any event, where an event is an arrival or an activation of a stop (Proposition 2.2).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "FC1-c100", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Which orders' shares count in the submitted total G?", "back": "Each market order at its arrival, each limit order at its arrival and each stop at its activation. Stops that have not activated are not counted (Proposition 2.3).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "FC1-c109", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What is settlement?", "back": "The transfer of cash and instruments that discharges the obligations that clearing has fixed (Definition 1.1)."}
{"id": "FC1-c101", "lesson": "L05", "front": "Under A1, how do net payments compare with gross payments?", "back": "Under A1, each buyer and seller of a trade differ and its quantity and price are positive integers. Then 0 ≤ NP ≤ G, so net payments are non-negative and at most gross payments, where G is the sum of the considerations (Proposition 2.1(ii)).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "FC1-c102", "lesson": "L05", "front": "Under A1 and A2, what is each participant's net obligation to the CCP?", "back": "Its batch net position x_j: the CCP owes j the amount x_j when x_j > 0, and j owes the CCP −x_j when x_j < 0 (Proposition 2.2).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "FC1-c114", "lesson": "L05", "front": "How are the gross payments of a batch defined?", "back": "Gross payments are G = Σ c_i, the sum of the considerations of all trades in the batch (Section 2)."}
{"id": "FC1-c118", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What does the offsets_nondecreasing line of Lab 1 check?", "back": "It checks that the business-day offsets of the six lifecycle states never decrease along the lifecycle (Definition 1.2; Lab 1)."}
{"id": "FC1-c119", "lesson": "L04", "front": "When does a last trade price exist, and what does it govern?", "back": "The last trade price is the price of the most recent fill. Before the first fill there is none, so no stop is active (Remark 1.2)."}
{"id": "FC1-c121", "lesson": "L04", "front": "When does a sell stop activate?", "back": "A sell stop activates when the last trade price is at or below its stop price. On activation it becomes a market order for its full quantity (Definition 1.1)."}
{"id": "FC1-c123", "lesson": "L05", "front": "How is a participant's net position in a batch defined?", "back": "Participant j's net position is x_j = Rec_j − Pay_j, its receipts minus its payments, where Rec_j sums the considerations of the trades j sells and Pay_j those it buys (Section 2)."}
{"id": "FC1-c124", "lesson": "L05", "front": "How are the net payments of a batch defined?", "back": "Net payments are NP = Σ_j max(−x_j, 0), summed over participants: each participant with a negative net position contributes the size of that position (Section 2)."}
{"id": "FC1-c132", "lesson": "L04", "front": "What is the best bid?", "back": "The highest price among the resting bid orders (Remark 1.2; Definition 2.1)."}
{"id": "FC1-c133", "lesson": "L04", "front": "What is the best ask?", "back": "The lowest price among the resting ask orders (Remark 1.2; Definition 2.1)."}
{"id": "FC1-c135", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Does share conservation have a term for resting shares withdrawn from the book?", "back": "No. Under Assumption A2 no resting order is withdrawn or changed except by trading, so Proposition 2.3 has no term for shares removed from the book by withdrawal (Assumption A2; Proposition 2.3)."}
{"id": "FC1-c141", "lesson": "L04", "front": "When more than one pending stop triggers, in what order do they activate?", "back": "In arrival order, one at a time (Remark 1.2)."}
{"id": "FC1-c142", "lesson": "L05", "front": "When does the offsets_nondecreasing line of Lab 1 print False?", "back": "When the clearing offset is larger than the settlement lag (Section 1; Lab 1)."}
{"id": "FC1-c143", "lesson": "L04", "front": "What is a resting order?", "back": "The unfilled part of a limit order, waiting in the book at its price (Remark 1.2)."}
{"id": "FC1-c144", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What is netting?", "back": "Replacing the gross amounts of a batch with each participant's net position (Definition 1.1; Section 2)."}
{"id": "FC1-c145", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What is novation?", "back": "Where a CCP is used, replacing a trade between the two original parties with two trades: one between the buyer and the CCP and one between the seller and the CCP (Definition 1.1)."}
{"id": "FC1-c154", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Does an earlier fill at 1010 trigger a buy stop at 1005 when the arriving order's last fill is at 1000?", "back": "No. Only the last trade price at the check counts, which here is 1000, below the stop of 1005 (Remark 1.2)."}
{"id": "FC1-c155", "lesson": "L04", "front": "After a stop activates, does the stop check run again?", "back": "Yes. Each activation can move the last trade price, so the check repeats until no pending stop triggers (Remark 1.2)."}
{"id": "FC1-c156", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Under Assumption A2 of the order book, which shares can be cancelled?", "back": "Only the unfilled shares of market orders (Assumption A2; Definition 1.1)."}
{"id": "FC1-c164", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What is the trade date?", "back": "The business day on which the trade is matched (Definition 1.2)."}
{"id": "FC1-c165", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What is the clearing offset?", "back": "The business-day offset of the clearing state from the trade date (Definition 1.2). The lab sets it to 0."}
{"id": "FC1-c166", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What is the settlement lag?", "back": "The business-day offset of the settlement state from the trade date (Definition 1.2). The lab sets it to 2 business days."}
{"id": "FC1-c167", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What is the net-to-gross ratio of a batch?", "back": "Net payments divided by gross payments, on the gross basis (Section 2)."}
{"id": "FC1-c168", "lesson": "L05", "front": "When is a participant a net payer?", "back": "When its net position is negative, x_j < 0 (Section 2)."}
{"id": "FC1-c169", "lesson": "L04", "front": "After a market buy of 150 shares takes 60 shares at 1000 cents and 90 shares at 1100 cents, how many shares remain on the ask side at 1100 cents?", "back": "10 shares. The ask at 1100 held 100 shares and 90 were filled (Example 1.3).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "FC1-c171", "lesson": "L04", "front": "What is the average price in cents of the market buy of 150 shares that meets 60 shares at 1000 cents and 100 shares at 1100 cents?", "back": "1060 cents: the cost of 159000 cents over 150 shares (Example 1.3; Lab 1, Fixture A).", "grade": "T"}
{"id": "FC1-c172", "lesson": "L05", "front": "What is the consideration of the Lab 1 trade, in integer cents?", "back": "2172960 cents, which is 216 times 10060 (Lab 1, consideration_cents). SIMULATED.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (SIMULATED)"}
